WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.640 --> 00:00:09.920
Hey guys, Helen here, Wisecrack's resident
science nerd.

00:00:09.920 --> 00:00:14.129
While 14-year-old Jared was waiting in line
for 7 hours early for The Matrix: Reloaded, I

00:00:14.129 --> 00:00:18.580
was playing with my Star Wars action figures,
acting out weird love triangles between Qui-Gon,

00:00:18.580 --> 00:00:22.740
Jango, and Princess Leia and trying to convince
my family that my home planet was actually

00:00:22.740 --> 00:00:23.740
Alderaan.

00:00:23.740 --> 00:00:28.109
So, I decided to flex my Star Wars Geekery
for this discussion of Disney's latest installment

00:00:28.109 --> 00:00:31.050
in the Star Wars Cinematic Universe: Solo.

00:00:31.050 --> 00:00:35.489
The movie fills in the backstory of our favorite
smuggler-turned-hero, showing us how a young

00:00:35.489 --> 00:00:40.329
Han transforms into the legendary Han Solo…
sort of.

00:00:40.329 --> 00:00:44.809
Given that the other films have been massive
cash cows, Solo was expected to do pretty

00:00:44.809 --> 00:00:49.280
well, even considering the Last Jedi backlash
and potential franchise fatigue.

00:00:49.280 --> 00:00:50.920
But Solo flopped.

00:00:50.920 --> 00:00:54.620
It’s on track to be the first unprofitable
Star Wars film ever.

00:00:54.620 --> 00:00:59.519
This should surprise you for one reason: everything
about this movie is designed to be marketable

00:00:59.519 --> 00:01:01.079
and give you the warm fuzzies.

00:01:01.079 --> 00:01:03.190
So, what went wrong?

00:01:03.190 --> 00:01:08.090
Let’s take a look in this Wisecrack Edition
on how even psychology couldn’t sell Solo.

00:01:08.090 --> 00:01:12.360
Thanks to our sponsors at Brilliant.org for
making this video possible, and as always,

00:01:12.360 --> 00:01:14.140
spoilers ahead.

00:01:14.140 --> 00:01:20.390
Solo kicks off sometime around the year 13
BBY, that’s 3264 on the Lothal calendar,

00:01:20.390 --> 00:01:23.650
and ends roughly 10 years before Rogue One
and A New Hope.

00:01:23.650 --> 00:01:28.340
The movie follows aspiring pilot and scoundrel-to-be
Han, as he goes from a bright-eyed romantic

00:01:28.340 --> 00:01:30.770
to an outlaw who shoots first.

00:01:30.770 --> 00:01:34.480
“Sorry about the mess.”

00:01:34.480 --> 00:01:39.450
Along the way, he picks up a lovable 196-year-old
Wookiee, a notorious technosexual smuggler

00:01:39.450 --> 00:01:43.280
and the Millennium Falcon, though he doesn’t
quite pull off the Kessel run in less than

00:01:43.280 --> 00:01:45.630
12 parsecs - unless you round down.

00:01:45.630 --> 00:01:50.090
Here’s Solo’s fundamental strategy: make
us nostalgic for the good ol’ days of Star

00:01:50.090 --> 00:01:54.799
Wars by cramming in as many easter eggs and
nods to the past Star Wars as possible.

00:01:54.799 --> 00:01:59.360
There’s the golden dice, the planet Felucia,
viper droids, Mandalorian armor, Twi'leks,

00:01:59.360 --> 00:02:03.660
Jabba the Hutt, Bossk the Bounty Hunter, colo
clawfish, and of course, Darth Maul.

00:02:03.660 --> 00:02:05.880
And that’s just scratching the surface.

00:02:05.880 --> 00:02:10.890
The movie is one easter egg after the next,
pulling references not only from the films,

00:02:10.890 --> 00:02:14.940
but also from the novelizations, the video
games, and the animated series.

00:02:14.940 --> 00:02:19.090
The reference to Aurra Sing, Lando’s disguise
from Return of the Jedi and Qi’ra’s impressive

00:02:19.090 --> 00:02:23.560
command of Teräs Käsi are great ways to
make fans who know two generations-worth of

00:02:23.560 --> 00:02:27.060
Star Wars lore feel like they have a special
connection to the film.

00:02:27.060 --> 00:02:31.640
But despite the outstanding fan service and
some great deep cuts, the movie falls short

00:02:31.640 --> 00:02:36.290
because nostalgia alone can’t propel a film,
and might even get in the way.

00:02:36.290 --> 00:02:40.590
While I can’t read Disney’s mind, the
reason they relied on all this nostalgia seems

00:02:40.590 --> 00:02:42.300
pretty… obvious.

00:02:42.300 --> 00:02:46.310
Marketers use nostalgia to sell stuff all
the time, and it’s no different in movies.

00:02:46.310 --> 00:02:50.709
Just look at the Force Awakens: besides bringing
back the actual characters - and actors - from

00:02:50.709 --> 00:02:54.550
the original trilogy, the film paid homage
to its predecessors in the form of things

00:02:54.550 --> 00:02:59.209
like trash compactors, podracing flags and
that even that werewolf alien thing from the

00:02:59.209 --> 00:03:00.209
Mos Eisley cantina.

00:03:00.209 --> 00:03:04.940
Neuroscience has a possible explanation for
why this trick could be super effective: it

00:03:04.940 --> 00:03:07.420
involves the brain's reward system.

00:03:07.420 --> 00:03:11.019
Some imaging studies have shown that the parts
of the brain tied to feelings like pleasure,

00:03:11.019 --> 00:03:14.629
motivation, and euphoria are activated when
you’re experiencing nostalgia.

00:03:14.629 --> 00:03:19.220
So, for long-time Star Wars fans, seeing things
like Han’s dice and hearing the sound of

00:03:19.220 --> 00:03:24.120
the imperial fighters are like a jump to hyperspace
for the feel-good pathways in your brain - which

00:03:24.120 --> 00:03:28.700
is exactly what Disney wants to happen, because
then you’ll keep coming back for more.

00:03:28.700 --> 00:03:31.660
Or buy some Solo dice cufflinks and a Kessel
run T-shirt.

00:03:31.660 --> 00:03:35.409
But nostalgia marketing aside, these easter
eggs do something else.

00:03:35.409 --> 00:03:40.239
In the case of humor, being on the receiving
end of an inside joke makes us feel more affiliation

00:03:40.239 --> 00:03:43.900
with the jokester and makes them seem more
"socially desirable".

00:03:43.900 --> 00:03:48.110
"I do not understand-" "I do!

00:03:48.110 --> 00:03:50.020
I understood that reference."

00:03:50.020 --> 00:03:53.890
And according to the encryption theory of
humor, jokes are funny not just because of

00:03:53.890 --> 00:03:57.950
their superficial content, but because they
broadcast shared knowledge between the funny

00:03:57.950 --> 00:03:59.640
person and their audience.

00:03:59.640 --> 00:04:03.760
So, if you caught yourself laughing in the
scene where Chewie’s losing it over being

00:04:03.760 --> 00:04:07.410
beaten at dejarik or the one where he’s
holding up the guard’s severed arms, the

00:04:07.410 --> 00:04:11.480
writers did their job - from a psychology
standpoint, the fact that Disney shares your

00:04:11.480 --> 00:04:15.970
“inside knowledge” that Wookiees rip peoples’
arms off makes you feel like they get it.

00:04:15.970 --> 00:04:18.480
"But sir, nobody worries about upsetting a
droid."

00:04:18.480 --> 00:04:23.200
"That's ‘cause a droid don't pull people's
arms out of their sockets if they loose.

00:04:23.200 --> 00:04:25.480
Wookiees are known to do that."

00:04:25.480 --> 00:04:27.320
But here’s where Solo lands itself in trouble.

00:04:27.320 --> 00:04:33.270
Plenty of movies have inside jokes and references
while also giving us well-developed characters.

00:04:33.270 --> 00:04:38.170
But in the process of doubling-down on nostalgic
easter eggs, Disney fails to give us a legit

00:04:38.170 --> 00:04:39.300
origin story.

00:04:39.300 --> 00:04:43.850
Solo’s fundamental problem is that Alden
Ehrenreich has to convince us he’s Harrison

00:04:43.850 --> 00:04:49.140
Ford’s Han Solo from the very start: he’s
got the arrogance, the wisecracks, the hustle

00:04:49.140 --> 00:04:53.790
— Han’s supposed transition is basically
75 percent complete right from the beginning.

00:04:53.790 --> 00:04:58.600
So, by the time he shoots Beckett at the end,
it doesn’t seem like much of a change - he’s

00:04:58.600 --> 00:05:01.550
been acting like Harrison Ford the whole time.

00:05:01.550 --> 00:05:05.740
The film makes gestures of a transformation,
but they seem half-hearted when crammed between

00:05:05.740 --> 00:05:07.920
8,000 other nostalgia triggers.

00:05:07.920 --> 00:05:12.330
As a result, for a film that promises to showcase
the development of one of the most beloved

00:05:12.330 --> 00:05:18.440
characters in sci-fi history, it feels more
like a Buzzfeed top 10 list of Han Solo trivia.

00:05:18.440 --> 00:05:23.400
Even significant plot points - like the way
Han gets his last name, meets Lando or wins

00:05:23.400 --> 00:05:26.590
the Millennium Falcon - feel like easter eggs.

00:05:26.590 --> 00:05:30.330
Take Hans relationship with Chewie: Han’s
only known him for a couple minutes before

00:05:30.330 --> 00:05:34.730
their relationship has the same dynamic as
it does throughout the rest of the saga.

00:05:34.730 --> 00:05:39.810
Normally, we’d expect this kind of connection
to develop and mature over time, but because

00:05:39.810 --> 00:05:44.900
Disney’s top priority was to trigger nostalgia
and familiarity rather than truly show development,

00:05:44.900 --> 00:05:49.520
it ends up feeling shallow - the movie gets
it out of the way and moves on to the next

00:05:49.520 --> 00:05:50.920
bit of trivia.

00:05:50.920 --> 00:05:55.430
The point of an origin story is to show us
how a character we know and love comes to

00:05:55.430 --> 00:05:56.730
be who they are.

00:05:56.730 --> 00:06:01.650
For instance, Batman Begins shows Bruce Wayne
mastering his fears to become the legendary

00:06:01.650 --> 00:06:02.910
Dark Knight.

00:06:02.910 --> 00:06:07.371
Bruce starts out as a troubled, angsty, scared
youth at the beginning, and by the end he

00:06:07.371 --> 00:06:09.470
is disciplined and unafraid.

00:06:09.470 --> 00:06:13.820
Doctor Strange starts off as a self-centered
surgeon who only believes in cold rationality,

00:06:13.820 --> 00:06:17.120
and ends the film with him as a self-sacrificial
mystic.

00:06:17.120 --> 00:06:18.810
But to be honest, I kind of get it.

00:06:18.810 --> 00:06:23.500
When you’re re-casting such an iconic role,
you get stuck between a rock and a hard place.

00:06:23.500 --> 00:06:27.610
If Han’s antics at the beginning seemed
completely different than Harrison Ford, the

00:06:27.610 --> 00:06:29.700
audience wouldn’t believe they were watching
Han Solo.

00:06:29.700 --> 00:06:34.430
But if you have him act like Ford’s Solo
from the beginning, you sacrifice a convincing

00:06:34.430 --> 00:06:36.080
arc for familiarity.

00:06:36.080 --> 00:06:40.420
If we were to try to give the film more credit,
we might say that the arc is Han going from

00:06:40.420 --> 00:06:45.160
an aspiring scoundrel to an idealist when
he decides to hand over the Hyperfuel to the

00:06:45.160 --> 00:06:46.160
budding resistance.

00:06:46.160 --> 00:06:49.410
But isn’t that the exact same arc from A
New Hope?

00:06:49.410 --> 00:06:51.650
“Hey, I knew there was more to you than
money!"

00:06:51.650 --> 00:06:53.460
At worst, the film has no arc.

00:06:53.460 --> 00:06:55.520
And at best, it’s a retread of Episode 4.

00:06:55.520 --> 00:06:59.390
That’s not to say Solo doesn’t have moments
of fun, but that’s really the best thing

00:06:59.390 --> 00:07:00.590
you could say about it.

00:07:00.590 --> 00:07:04.110
And hey, if that’s what you’re looking
for, it’s a good time.

00:07:04.110 --> 00:07:08.620
But in our opinion, Disney could have done
better by delivering an intriguing character

00:07:08.620 --> 00:07:33.430
rather than relying on cheap appeals to nostalgia
in the form of endless

00:07:33.430 --> 00:07:51.140
easter eggs.

00:07:51.140 --> 00:08:18.480
But that’s our take - leave us yours in
the comments below.

